\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:3.1.1}
If $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ is the function $f(x) = x^2 - 4$, then$$\lim_{x \to 1} f(x) = f(1) = 1 - 4 = -3$$since $f$ is continuous.
\end{proposition}

proof:
\begin{proof}
Given: Let $\varepsilon > 0$ be arbitrary.Choice of $\delta$: Choose $\delta = \min\left(1, \frac{\varepsilon}{3}\right)$.Verification: Suppose $0 < |x - 1| < \delta$. Then:Since $\delta \le 1$, we have $|x - 1| < 1$, which implies $|x + 1| < 3$.Since $\delta \le \frac{\varepsilon}{3}$, we have:$$|f(x) - (-3)| = |x^2 - 1| = |x - 1||x + 1| < \delta \cdot 3 \le \left(\frac{\varepsilon}{3}\right) \cdot 3 = \varepsilon$$Thus, by Definition 3.1, $\lim_{x \to 1} f(x) = -3$
\end{proof}

