\begin{prop}
\label{prop:3.2.3}
for any fixed $x \in \mathbb{R}$, the sequence $f^{(n)}(x) = \frac{x}{n}$ converges to $L = 0$ as $n \to \infty$
\end{prop}

proof:
\begin{proof}
Proof:Given: Let $\varepsilon > 0$ be any arbitrary positive real number.Goal: We must find a threshold $N$ (analogous to $1/\delta$ in the $n \to \infty$ context) such that for all $n > N$, the distance $d_Y(f^{(n)}(x), 0) < \varepsilon$.Analysis:We examine the distance in the metric space $(\mathbb{R}, d_{Euclidean})$:$$d_Y(f^{(n)}(x), 0) = \left| \frac{x}{n} - 0 \right| = \frac{|x|}{n}$$We want $\frac{|x|}{n} < \varepsilon$. Solving for $n$, we get $n > \frac{|x|}{\varepsilon}$.Selection of Threshold: By the Archimedean Property, there exists an integer $N$ such that $N > \frac{|x|}{\varepsilon}$.Verification: For all $n \in \mathbb{N}$, if $n > N$, then:$$d_Y(f^{(n)}(x), 0) = \frac{|x|}{n} < \frac{|x|}{N} < \frac{|x|}{|x|/\varepsilon} = \varepsilon$$This satisfies the condition in Definition 3.1.
\end{proof}

