\begin{prop}
\label{prop:3.2.4}
Let $f^{(n)}: [0, 1] \to \mathbb{R}$ be defined by $f^{(n)}(x) := x^n$. The pointwise limit $f: [0, 1] \to \mathbb{R}$ is defined as:$$f(x) := \begin{cases} 0 & \text{if } 0 \le x < 1 \\ 1 & \text{if } x = 1 \end{cases}$$
\end{prop}

proof:
\begin{proof}
Proof of Pointwise ConvergenceCase $x = 1$: $f^{(n)}(1) = 1^n = 1$ for all $n$. Thus, $\lim_{n \to \infty} f^{(n)}(1) = 1 = f(1)$.Case $0 \le x < 1$: Since $x$ is a fixed real number less than $1$, the geometric sequence $x^n$ converges to $0$. Thus, $\lim_{n \to \infty} f^{(n)}(x) = 0 = f(x)$
Proof of Discontinuity of the Limit Function $f$While each $f^{(n)}(x) = x^n$ is continuous on $[0,1]$, we show $f$ is discontinuous at $x_0 = 1$ using the limit definition:Evaluate the limit as $x \to 1$ along $E = [0, 1]$:For any $x \in [0, 1)$, $f(x) = 0$. Therefore, $\lim_{x \to 1; x \in [0, 1)} f(x) = 0$.Compare with the function value:$f(1) = 1$.Conclusion:Since $\lim_{x \to 1} f(x) = 0 \neq 1 = f(1)$, the function $f$ is not continuous at $x=1$.
\end{proof}

