\begin{prop}
\label{prop:3.2.6}
Suppose that $f^{(n)} : [a, b] \to \mathbb{R}$ is a sequence of Riemann-integrable functions on the interval $[a, b]$. If $\int_{[a, b]} f^{(n)} = L$ for every $n$, and $f^{(n)}$ converges pointwise to some new function $f$, this does not mean that $\int_{[a, b]} f = L$. An example comes by setting $[a, b] := [0, 1]$, and letting $f^{(n)}$ be the function $f^{(n)}(x) := 2n$ when $x \in [1/2n, 1/n]$, and $f^{(n)}(x) := 0$ for all other values of $x$. Then $f^{(n)}$ converges pointwise to the zero function $f(x) := 0$. On the other hand, $\int_{[0, 1]} f^{(n)} = 1$ for every $n$, while $\int_{[0, 1]} f = 0$. In particular, we have an example where$$\lim_{n \to \infty} \int_{[a, b]} f^{(n)} \neq \int_{[a, b]} \lim_{n \to \infty} f^{(n)} \text{.}$$
\end{prop}

proof:
\begin{proof}
Proof and Analysis of Example 3.2.6This example demonstrates that pointwise convergence does not preserve the integral; specifically, you cannot generally swap a limit and an integral.1. Integral of each $f^{(n)}$For each $n$, the function $f^{(n)}$ is a "spike" of height $2n$ over an interval of width $1/n - 1/2n = 1/2n$.Calculation:$$\int_0^1 f^{(n)}(x) \, dx = \int_{1/2n}^{1/n} 2n \, dx = 2n \times \left( \frac{1}{n} - \frac{1}{2n} \right) = 2n \times \frac{1}{2n} = 1 \text{.}$$Thus, the sequence of integrals is constant: $(1, 1, 1, \dots)$, so $\lim_{n \to \infty} \int f^{(n)} = 1$.2. Pointwise Limit $f$We must show $f^{(n)}(x) \to 0$ for every $x \in [0, 1]$.Case $x = 0$: For all $n$, $f^{(n)}(0) = 0$ (since $0$ is never in $[1/2n, 1/n]$). Thus, $\lim_{n \to \infty} f^{(n)}(0) = 0$.Case $x > 0$: As $n$ increases, the interval $[1/2n, 1/n]$ shrinks and moves toward the origin. Eventually, for a large enough $N$, $1/N < x$. For all $n > N$, $x$ will lie to the right of the interval $[1/2n, 1/n]$, meaning $f^{(n)}(x) = 0$.Thus, $f^{(n)} \to f$ pointwise, where $f(x) = 0$ for all $x$.3. Integral of the LimitThe integral of the zero function $f$ is:$$\int_0^1 f(x) \, dx = \int_0^1 0 \, dx = 0 \text{.}$$III. ConclusionSince $1 \neq 0$, this confirms that pointwise convergence is insufficient to guarantee that the limit of the integrals equals the integral of the limit. This serves as the primary motivation for studying uniform convergence, which does allow for this interchange.
\end{proof}

